[test-suite name=io
  [doc [#
conformance fixtures for cx-stdlib/io (spec/std-lib/io.md §6).

   Each program imports the bundled module via [?lib 'cx-stdlib/io']
   (resolver MUST be quoted) then calls [$io:fn args]. The LAST
   expression is the rendered result.

   DETERMINISM / EFFECTFUL MODULE: cx-stdlib/io is a Tier-B, necessarily
   impure module — EVERY public function is `impure` (§3) and every one
   of its effect points is capability-gated (§7). The stdlib eval runner
   evaluates each case under `code.new_env()`, which grants NO
   capabilities (deny-by-default per spec/core/security.md §2). At the
   effect point the active set is checked FIRST; with the grant absent
   the function raises E_CAP_DENIED = `cx-err:CXER0271` (security.md §4:
   "a denied effect at the effect point raises CXER0271"; io.md §7:
   effectful functions "raise cx-err:CXER0271 when the grant is absent").

   Consequently the deterministic, runner-faithful, spec-grounded
   assertion for the happy-path call of every io function is the
   capability-denied error CXER0271 — real side effects (touching the
   filesystem, opening fds, deleting trees) are neither reproducible nor
   safe inside the conformance harness, and AUTHORING.md ("Capability-
   gated / effectful") directs us to fixture the capability-denied error
   rather than real side effects. Each public function therefore gets a
   CXER0271 case exercising its real call shape (correct arity / arg
   kinds per the §3 [?def] signature).

   IO-SPECIFIC ERROR CODES (CXER3400–CXER3411, §5): these are documented
   behaviors that fire only AFTER the capability check passes (a granted
   read/write that then hits invalid-UTF-8, not-found, permission, a
   root-delete guard, etc.). Under the runner's empty capability set the
   capability check short-circuits FIRST, so a correct implementation
   raises CXER0271 — NOT the io-specific code — for every one of these
   programs. Asserting CXER3400/3401/3402/3410/3411 here would therefore
   FAIL a correct implementation (a wrong fixture is worse than a missing
   one, AUTHORING.md "Grounding"). They are intentionally NOT asserted as
   out-err in this suite; their scenarios are documented in the spec's §6
   conformance list and belong in a capability-granted harness
   (conformance/stdlib/io.cxd) once the host-grant fixture surface exists. See
   the report's `ambiguous` list.

   NOTE: no inter-fixture comment blocks appear between cases (a comment
   between sections risks capture into the preceding section payload);
   all explanatory prose lives in this doc header or in tags.
#]]

 [case id=io-001-read-file-cap-denied level=core
  [tags io read-file capability CXER0271]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/io']
[$io:read-file "/var/log/app.log"]
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0271
#]]
 ]

